"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,330\nFirst in the family\n\n4\n00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:13,973\nFirst generation college students,\n\n5\n00:00:13,993 --> 00:00:16,187\nwhose parents did not earn a degree,\n\n6\n00:00:16,314 --> 00:00:19,038\narrive just as capable as their peers.\n\n7\n00:00:19,341 --> 00:00:22,248\nBut many have never been told the unwritten rules.\n\n8\n00:00:22,585 --> 00:00:24,320\nWhat office hours are for.\n\n9\n00:00:24,645 --> 00:00:26,584\nThat asking for help is normal.\n\n10\n00:00:26,863 --> 00:00:28,526\nHow to talk to a professor.\n\n11\n00:00:28,696 --> 00:00:30,712\nAt a selective private university,\n\n12\n00:00:30,982 --> 00:00:35,842\nresearchers ran a one hour panel for a hundred and forty seven incoming students.\n\n13\n00:00:36,247 --> 00:00:41,098\nIn both versions, senior students shared stories about adjusting to college.\n\n14\n00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,433\nIn the difference education version,\n\n15\n00:00:43,669 --> 00:00:48,090\nthey also explained how their social class backgrounds shaped those stories.\n\n16\n00:00:48,577 --> 00:00:52,615\nOne panelist said that because their parents had not gone to college,\n\n17\n00:00:52,869 --> 00:00:55,986\nthe family could not always give the advice they needed.\n\n18\n00:00:56,156 --> 00:00:57,519\nIn the standard version,\n\n19\n00:00:57,763 --> 00:01:03,384\nfirst generation students ended the year with grades about point three lower than their peers.\n\n20\n00:01:03,842 --> 00:01:08,097\nThe difference education panel closed that gap by sixty three percent.\n\n21\n00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:09,258\nWhy did it work?\n\n22\n00:01:09,689 --> 00:01:13,884\nFirst generation students who heard it sought out more campus resources,\n\n23\n00:01:14,077 --> 00:01:17,211\nand that help seeking accounted for their higher grades.\n\n24\n00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:19,940\nHearing how people like them had struggled,\n\n25\n00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,992\nand coped, made asking for help feel normal.\n\n26\n00:01:23,162 --> 00:01:26,130\nContinuing generation students benefited too.\n\n27\n00:01:26,632 --> 00:01:28,761\nThey reported better well being,\n\n28\n00:01:28,925 --> 00:01:30,756\nengagement, and social fit,\n\n29\n00:01:31,077 --> 00:01:34,043\nand a deeper appreciation of different backgrounds.\n\n30\n00:01:34,213 --> 00:01:35,185\nSome caution.\n\n31\n00:01:35,612 --> 00:01:37,267\nThis was one university,\n\n32\n00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:39,786\none cohort, and a modest sample.\n\n33\n00:01:40,098 --> 00:01:44,406\nAnd it worked best where real resources existed for students to use.\n\n34\n00:01:44,576 --> 00:01:45,370\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:45,671 --> 00:01:47,223\nName the hidden curriculum.\n\n36\n00:01:47,503 --> 00:01:49,452\nExplain what office hours are for,\n\n37\n00:01:49,472 --> 00:01:50,819\nand how to use them.\n\n38\n00:01:51,203 --> 00:01:53,634\nShare stories, yours or former students',\n\n39\n00:01:53,654 --> 00:01:55,321\nincluding what was hard.\n\n40\n00:01:55,599 --> 00:01:57,459\nAnd make help seeking routine,\n\n41\n00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:58,516\nnot remedial.\n\n42\n00:01:58,939 --> 00:02:00,788\nOne tip to share with students: ask about the unwritten rules,\n\n43\n00:02:01,018 --> 00:02:04,853\nlike how office hours work.\n\n44\n00:02:05,023 --> 00:02:06,851\nDon't hide the rules of college.\n\n45\n00:02:06,977 --> 00:02:07,825\nTeach them.\n\n"
